#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073620 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073680 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c16dc76e-e756-5f66-9453-ba88b3f37f67" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Trigonometric Ratio"}] :question/id #uuid "2b0df0b9-3484-5408-aadc-01e756c43de7" :question/math-academy-id "e-3315" :question/problem "Given that $x=0.3$ radians, what is $\\sin{x}$ rounded to $3$ decimal places?" :question/worked-solution "Note the argument of the sine is given in radians. Using a calculator in \"radians\" mode, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\sin(0.3) = 0.295\\,520\\ldots \\approx 0.296\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073621 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073681 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a2e44aa-d511-5bb4-bfe3-3213d7df0f1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Trigonometric Ratio of an Acute Special Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "b9829363-8cde-55fb-a1de-b6460ef451a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-3317" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)$ by converting the angle to degrees." :question/worked-solution "First, we convert $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ radians to an equivalent measure in degrees. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi} 6\\right) \\left(\\dfrac {180^\\circ} {\\pi}\\right) = \\dfrac {180^\\circ} {6} = 30^\\circ.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right) = \\sin 30^\\circ.\n$$\n\nFinally, since we know that $\\sin 30^\\circ = \\dfrac{1}{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left( \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)  = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073622 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073682 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2439bd5-8e25-5e4f-b852-72cabeeb440f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Trigonometric Ratio"}] :question/id #uuid "55d74eac-00aa-54cc-8e1f-743e053cc599" :question/math-academy-id "e-3318" :question/problem "Rounded to three decimal places, what is $\\arcsin(0.75)$ in radians?" :question/worked-solution "Note that we need the answer in radians. Using a calculator in \"radians\" mode, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\arcsin(0.75) = 0.848\\,062\\ldots \\approx 0.848 \\, \\textrm{radians}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073623 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce9c6436-70ed-5b95-b336-b40f0931f825" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements"}] :question/id #uuid "7eadc0f5-b50a-5e8e-9706-7f4fe0554abf" :question/math-academy-id "e-3319" :question/problem "Is the following statement  true or false?\n\n$$\n\\sin30^\\circ \\:\\overset{\\large\\color{red}?}{=}\\: \\sin \\left(  \\dfrac16 \\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The statement is false. Converting $30^\\circ$ from degrees to radians, we get \n\n$$\n30^\\circ= 30\\cdot \\dfrac{\\pi}{180} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\, \\textrm{radians}.\n$$\n\nThe statement is false because $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\neq \\dfrac{1}6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073682 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2439bd5-8e25-5e4f-b852-72cabeeb440f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Trigonometric Ratio"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c3261b6-3ec8-5138-960e-090e166d83ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.644" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520783} {:answer/id #uuid "10fa8ad3-449b-5fca-9097-ecdf6c0d44a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.643" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520799} {:answer/id #uuid "1aabb5f6-195d-588b-b75e-6c1c6a8b2338" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.643" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520821} {:answer/id #uuid "c7e9e031-b3d5-5906-a23f-a324a3d38b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.464" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521210} {:answer/id #uuid "fef18c21-70a3-5857-af0d-6f8d7ed21a10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.364" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c3261b6-3ec8-5138-960e-090e166d83ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.644" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520783} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0522211-b80f-5c0f-b2da-70dcd1257145" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b89dfe3-da21-530c-903d-947ec6af3854" :question/math-academy-id "q-123503" :question/problem "Rounded to three decimal places, what is $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.8)$ in radians?" :question/worked-solution "Note that we need the answer in radians. Using a calculator in \"radians\" mode, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.8)=0.643\\,501…≈0.644\\,\\text{radians}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521498 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce9c6436-70ed-5b95-b336-b40f0931f825" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20b415d0-ce3e-5252-babf-70efd7ce0657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520831} {:answer/id #uuid "58c31464-2ecd-5861-b8f8-f20ce9749e20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520966} {:answer/id #uuid "78a7f04a-4b10-5b42-9c84-6a290d024e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521034} {:answer/id #uuid "c589619a-7081-5009-a45d-0f03134703e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521204} {:answer/id #uuid "d1368367-9652-53bc-8d18-404303318464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58c31464-2ecd-5861-b8f8-f20ce9749e20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520966} :answer-field/id #uuid "1af2aa0f-c2a3-543f-a83e-691c13f6818c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521338}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5168a992-4557-5020-911a-67c9df9985ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-123537" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{15}^{∘}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{20}^{∘}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{36})$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{35}^{∘}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{36})$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Converting ${15}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{15}^{∘}=15\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Converting ${20}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{20}^{∘}=20\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{\\pi }{9}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nThe statement is false because $\\frac{\\pi }{9}\\ne \\frac{5\\pi }{36}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Converting ${35}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get \n\n$$\n{35}^{∘}=35\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{7\\pi }{36}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is 'I and III only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521494 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce9c6436-70ed-5b95-b336-b40f0931f825" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24e8e760-7f22-552c-92c8-7d281f94ccea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520842} {:answer/id #uuid "2d802dae-c64e-582a-850a-c8d967eab5f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520860} {:answer/id #uuid "3917eb06-1a11-50d1-a6bb-6d7d1b2d7a44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520888} {:answer/id #uuid "3f23a7ae-da79-5087-902a-7b787f048816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520906} {:answer/id #uuid "4d275836-192b-5d91-b4e5-fe1fa63b7154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3917eb06-1a11-50d1-a6bb-6d7d1b2d7a44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520888} :answer-field/id #uuid "76bea3b5-fc60-57ec-bd8b-23889bb99a1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4cd34dd2-403c-55c2-9000-3c54447738a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-123545" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{57}^{∘}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{19\\pi }{60})$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{32}^{∘}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{8\\pi }{35})$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{14}^{∘}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{80})$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Converting ${57}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{57}^{∘}=57\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{19\\pi }{60}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Converting ${32}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{32}^{∘}=32\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{8\\pi }{45}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nThe statement is false because $\\frac{8\\pi }{45}\\ne \\frac{8\\pi }{35}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Converting ${14}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{14}^{∘}=14\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{7\\pi }{90}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nThe statement is false because $\\frac{7\\pi }{90}\\ne \\frac{7\\pi }{80}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is 'I only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521569 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce9c6436-70ed-5b95-b336-b40f0931f825" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fbc408b-c121-5287-963f-06a800c703d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520797} {:answer/id #uuid "132bb860-1537-5d50-8e8d-eab1cc552fe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520807} {:answer/id #uuid "3ee69202-d794-5dc7-b9e1-48471ca549b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520904} {:answer/id #uuid "8d9f692f-ebf1-58e1-8b6f-d036750bb7c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521085} {:answer/id #uuid "f63824fa-c71a-5d2f-8dd7-c1adb8d01387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f63824fa-c71a-5d2f-8dd7-c1adb8d01387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521304} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d98aaaa-2215-595e-9cfe-13f69f4453bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f7892f8a-0a7a-5c55-9033-6382c732bb3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-123546" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡{110}^{∘}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{18})$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡{140}^{∘}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{9})$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡{170}^{∘}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{17\\pi }{18})$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Converting ${110}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{110}^{∘}=110\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{11\\pi }{18}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Converting ${140}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{140}^{∘}=140\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{7\\pi }{9}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Converting ${170}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{170}^{∘}=170\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{17\\pi }{18}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce9c6436-70ed-5b95-b336-b40f0931f825" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15aac7dc-8cd1-5607-ba90-095a24d98488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520815} {:answer/id #uuid "35f3791a-7cf7-5558-b05a-1693353bfa6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520879} {:answer/id #uuid "3b71401b-cf88-5840-8c19-a4e492d787c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520895} {:answer/id #uuid "a8691977-29a4-53b2-bc0b-9e23c70a02aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521142} {:answer/id #uuid "ce511b28-cdc4-5fcb-a0b8-330fbc0beae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b71401b-cf88-5840-8c19-a4e492d787c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520895} :answer-field/id #uuid "bffaf27a-e722-5e41-898d-3903f71f347b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "299f3633-d5e0-546a-8fb6-eacb46c1b2dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-123549" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{20}^{∘}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{9})$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{50}^{∘}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{18})$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{80}^{∘}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{9})$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Converting ${20}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get \n\n$$\n{20}^{∘}=20\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{\\pi }{9}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Converting ${50}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get \n\n$$\n{50}^{∘}=50\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{5\\pi }{18}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Converting ${80}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get \n\n$$\n{80}^{∘}=80\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{4\\pi }{9}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nThe statement is false because $\\frac{4\\pi }{9}\\ne \\frac{2\\pi }{9}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is 'I and II only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521556 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce9c6436-70ed-5b95-b336-b40f0931f825" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "217fd35f-043a-51ea-bb88-98a6243cbd0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520832} {:answer/id #uuid "36627598-707e-5fca-8a0d-d812713fd5ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520881} {:answer/id #uuid "37962c12-aa55-552e-a5ef-02ce2e2500fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520885} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e8c220-fc4e-5e93-9ea0-f85a2d09e7b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521133} {:answer/id #uuid "b460d5d1-82bb-564e-b699-500fb25f2615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37962c12-aa55-552e-a5ef-02ce2e2500fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520885} :answer-field/id #uuid "afac089e-3d46-5d44-b08d-1f8fc4927c12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da03fb8c-a7ca-5696-a1f6-3635e0d03dad" :question/math-academy-id "q-123550" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡{22}^{∘}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{90})$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡{94}^{∘}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{37\\pi }{90})$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡{76}^{∘}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{19\\pi }{25})$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Converting ${22}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{22}^{∘}=22\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{11\\pi }{90}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Converting ${94}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{94}^{∘}=94\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{47\\pi }{90}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nThe statement is false because $\\frac{47\\pi }{90}\\ne \\frac{37\\pi }{90}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Converting ${76}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get \n\n$$\n{76}^{∘}=76\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{19\\pi }{45}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nThe statement is false because $\\frac{19\\pi }{45}\\ne \\frac{19\\pi }{25}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is 'I only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521519 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073680 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c16dc76e-e756-5f66-9453-ba88b3f37f67" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Trigonometric Ratio"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d5010b7-65a7-5065-b697-c52c89709072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.479" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520789} {:answer/id #uuid "7f896e63-a82f-5ffc-963e-76ccd9950c4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.999" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521056} {:answer/id #uuid "9ae5b58c